<section class="how">
    <h2>How to Use This BMR Calculator</h2>
    <p>Select your gender, enter your age, weight in kilograms, and height in centimeters. Click the button and the result shows how many calories your body burns if you were to rest completely for 24 hours. This is your BMR.</p>
    <p>BMR on its own is not a calorie target to eat at. Most people burn significantly more than their BMR through daily movement and exercise. The practical next step is multiplying your BMR by an activity factor to get your TDEE, which is the actual number relevant for setting a calorie goal. Our TDEE Calculator does this calculation automatically.</p>
</section>

<section class="formula">
    <h2>The Mifflin-St Jeor BMR Formula</h2>
    <p>This calculator uses the Mifflin-St Jeor equation, which was published in 1990 and remains the standard recommendation from most dietitian associations today. Studies comparing BMR formulas consistently show it performs better than the older Harris-Benedict equation for most body types.</p>
    <p><strong>For men:</strong> BMR = (10 x weight in kg) + (6.25 x height in cm) - (5 x age in years) + 5</p>
    <p><strong>For women:</strong> BMR = (10 x weight in kg) + (6.25 x height in cm) - (5 x age in years) - 161</p>
    <p>The difference between men and women in the formula reflects average variation in lean mass. Muscle tissue burns more calories at rest than fat tissue, which is why body composition is such a significant factor in resting metabolism.</p>
</section>

<section class="examples">
    <h2>BMR Examples</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Male, 28 years old, 75 kg, 175 cm:</strong> BMR = (750) + (1,093.75) - (140) + 5 = approximately 1,709 kcal per day.</li>
        <li><strong>Female, 35 years old, 60 kg, 163 cm:</strong> BMR = (600) + (1,018.75) - (175) - 161 = approximately 1,283 kcal per day.</li>
        <li><strong>Older male, 55 years old, 90 kg, 180 cm:</strong> BMR = (900) + (1,125) - (275) + 5 = approximately 1,755 kcal per day. Note that BMR declines naturally with age as muscle mass tends to decrease after 30.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

