<section class="faq">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <h3>Should I eat at my BMR to lose weight?</h3>
    <p>No, and this is a common mistake. Eating only at your BMR means consuming fewer calories than your body needs even just to function at rest. In practice you are always doing something, so your actual needs are higher. Most nutrition guidelines consider anything below 1,200 calories for women and 1,500 for men risky without direct medical supervision. A better approach is calculating your TDEE and eating 300 to 500 below that number.</p>

    <h3>Does BMR change as I age?</h3>
    <p>Yes, it tends to decrease over time. After age 30, BMR drops roughly one to two percent per decade as muscle mass naturally declines. People who maintain muscle through resistance training tend to have higher BMRs at older ages. Significant weight loss also lowers BMR because the body adapts to carrying less mass.</p>

    <h3>Why does my BMR differ from another calculator?</h3>
    <p>Different tools use different formulas. Older calculators often rely on the Harris-Benedict equation from 1919, which tends to overestimate BMR compared to modern measurements. Mifflin-St Jeor is the current clinical standard. Slight differences between calculators using the same formula come down to rounding.</p>

    <h3>How is BMR different from metabolism?</h3>
    <p>Metabolism is a broad term covering all chemical processes your body performs. BMR is one specific measurable component of metabolism: the energy cost of basic life support at rest. People who say they have a "slow metabolism" often mean their BMR is relatively low compared to others of similar size, which can happen due to genetics, age, or low muscle mass.</p>
</section>

